In above three methods, the LSO for the OS vector (LSO\_SP) or for the MA vector (LSO\_MPT and LSO\_RTT) are executed one at a time after assessing whether the corresponding probability is satisfied once the critical path is determined; i.e., if a random number rand is less than 0.5, the LSO\_SP for OS vector is selected; otherwise the LSO\_MPT or LSO\_RTT for the MA vector is selected. While performing the LSO for the MA vector, if the random number rand is less than 0.5, then LSO\_MPT is selected, and LSO\_ RTT otherwise.
Fig. 7 shows an example of the LSO based on the data presented in Section 3.2.2. The critical path is determined as $\large { I O _ { 4 1 } }  \large { O _ { 4 2 } }  \large { O _ { 2 2 ^ { - } } }$ $ O _ { 2 3 }  O _ { 2 4 } ]$ as shown in Fig. 7 (a). Moreover, two critical operation blocks $( / { O } _ { 4 1 } \to { O } _ { 4 2 } \to { O } _ { 2 2 } \jmath$ and $[ O _ { 2 3 }  O _ { 2 4 } ] )$ are found in the critical path. Assuming that the three methods of including the LSO\_SP, LSO\_MPT, and LSO\_RTT are performed for the local search based on the critical path and blocks, the three corresponding results are as given in Fig. 7 (b), (c), and (d), respectively. It can be seen that the makespan is decreased from 33 to 30, 28, and 32 after using LSO\_SP, LSO\_MPT, and LSO\_RTT, respectively.
